course: dictionary
lesson: 10643
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can do more than one job. Uniform is a noun and an adjective. As a noun, a uniform is a set of special clothes that a group wears so they look the same or can be recognised. «A girl in Florida posted a photo in her school uniform» means she was wearing her school clothes.
explain: As an adjective, uniform means the same all over, or all the same. Test it by putting "the same" in its place: a uniform size is a size that is the same for everything. In maths and science, uniform can mean even throughout, as in a uniform speed that does not change.
explain: Uniforms help us recognise people who can help. «If you are lost in a town or a shop, you ask a shop worker, a police officer, a worker in uniform such as a lifeguard, or a parent with children for help.» A uniform shows someone's job, but it does not prove they are safe, so check with a trusted adult if you are unsure.
fact: A uniform (noun) is a set of special clothes that a group wears so they look the same or can be recognised.
fact: Uniform (adjective) means the same all over, or all the same: a uniform colour, a uniform size.
fact: Many schools in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand have uniforms; fewer do in the USA and Canada.
fact: Police officers, firefighters, nurses, paramedics and lifeguards wear uniforms so people can recognise them.
fact: A uniform alone does not prove someone is safe, so check with a trusted adult.
exercise: «A girl in Florida posted a photo in her school uniform.» In this sentence, what is a uniform? | A rule about homework | A kind of school bus | A photo of a street | *A set of special clothes that a school group wears
exercise: Uniform size means | Bigger than the size before | Very big | Only one size exists | *All the same size
exercise: «If you are lost, ask a worker in uniform, such as a lifeguard.» Why does the uniform help? | Uniforms make people invisible | Lifeguards are always wet | Uniforms are only for shops | *The uniform shows the person's job, so you can see who can help
test: In the sentence «They often quarrelled, like neighbouring schools with their own rules and uniforms», what does uniforms mean? | Lessons in maths | Neighbours who are kind | Things that are all the same colour | *Sets of special clothes worn by a group
test: Which sentence uses uniform as an adjective, meaning the same all over? | The police wore a uniform. | *The bricks were a uniform red all the way up the wall. | The uniform was washed on Monday. | Her uniform was hanging on the door.
exam: Mia says a stranger in a uniform must be safe. What is the best advice? | Tell the stranger your address | Ignore all adults in uniform | Trust anyone who wears a uniform | *A uniform does not prove someone is safe, so check with a trusted adult
